```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <markdown.h>

/**
 * Converts markdown text to HTML.
 *
 * @param markdownText A reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text.
 * @return A std::string containing html formatted text.
 */
std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdownText) {
    markdown::Markdown md;
    std::string htmlText = md.markdownToHtml(markdownText);
    return htmlText;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdownText = "# This is a heading\n\nThis is a paragraph with **bold** text.";
    std::string htmlText = markdownToHtml(markdownText);
    std::cout << htmlText << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

In this code, we use the markdown library to convert the markdown text to HTML. The `markdownToHtml` function takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown text, converts it to HTML using the `markdown::Markdown` class, and returns the result as a `std::string`.

Please note that you need to install the markdown library to use this code. You can install it using your package manager or by downloading it from the official markdown website.

Also, this code assumes that the markdown text is well-formed and does not contain any invalid markdown syntax. If you need to handle invalid markdown syntax, you may need to add error checking code to the `markdownToHtml` function.

